 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Architecture",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Inside are a regulator, an oscillator, photodiodes with a coating that gives an eye-like spectral response, ADCs for an ALS channel, a clear channel and temperature, upper and lower threshold comparators, interrupt logic and the I2C interface. Results are proportional to illuminance."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Start-up",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "After a power-on or a software reset the state machine starts; the oscillator runs, and the I2C address and trim values load from internal non-volatile memory; the part then idles in standby. Until the address is loaded it answers every I2C transfer with NACK."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Standby",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Standby is the state after power-up. The oscillator, internal support blocks and ADCs are off; I2C stays fully working."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Measurement",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Setting ALS_EN in MAIN_CTRL powers the support blocks; after they settle, typically 5 ms, conversions run at the programmed repeat rate. Clearing ALS_EN lets a conversion in progress finish, then the ADCs and support blocks return to standby. Writing MAIN_CTRL, ALS_MEAS_RATE or ALS_GAIN stops the running measurement and starts a new one."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Resolution and rate",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Resolution and integration time go together, from 13 bit at 3.125 ms to 20 bit at 400 ms; the repeat rate is set separately from 25 ms to 2000 ms. A repeat rate shorter than the conversion time is slowed to the fastest the conversion allows."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Interrupt",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "ALS_INT_EN = 1 turns the interrupt on. In threshold mode (ALS_VAR_MODE = 0) it sets once the result has left the band between the lower and upper thresholds for 1 + ALS_PERSIST consecutive results. In variance mode (ALS_VAR_MODE = 1) it sets when the result differs from the previous one by more than the variance threshold for 1 + ALS_PERSIST consecutive results. ALS_INT_SEL picks the ALS or clear channel as the source. INT is open drain."
  },
  {
   "topic": "I2C address and transfers",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Standard and fast mode I2C, one 7-bit slave address, 0x52; other addresses through the factory. Registers are read or written singly or in blocks. A block read skips reserved addresses and, past the last valid address, returns 00HEX without wrapping; a block write skips reserved and read-only registers, does not skip a register that mixes R and RW bits, and past the last valid address NACKs each further byte. Data sent to read-only bits has no effect. Starting a read on a non-readable register, or a write on a non-writable one, draws NACK until the transfer ends."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Data coherence",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "While an I2C read points into 07HEX to 18HEX, those registers are frozen; new results wait in shadow registers and are copied once the read ends or leaves that range. Only a block read guarantees that all bytes of a multi-byte result come from one conversion."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply bypass and pull-ups",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "A low-ESR, low-inductance ceramic bypass capacitor goes as close as possible to VDD, tied directly to the supply and to ground. SDA, SCL and INT each need a pull-up; 10 kΩ is typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Flicker rejection",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Integration times reject 50 Hz and 60 Hz flicker from mains-driven lamps, fluorescent tubes included."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Optical centre",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "The photodiode's active area is off the package centre: on APDS-9306 its centre sits (0.263) mm from the body centre toward pads 5 to 8, the area measuring (0.407) x (0.344) mm; on APDS-9306-065 it sits (0.223) mm to the side of centre."
  }
 ],
